# Kernel-domain policy for the zram writeback data path.
#
# post-fs-data.sh submits this file through ksud; a KernelSU build that loads
# module sepolicy.rule files itself may also pick it up, and applying a rule
# twice is a no-op on this ksud, so both paths are safe.
#
# Why the rule is needed: the loop worker is the side that reads and writes the
# zram backing file, and it runs as a kernel thread (u:r:kernel:s0) no matter
# which process attached the loop device.  Android's policy has no rule for that
# direction, so the first page of every writeback returns -EIO, the reserved
# block is freed again, and writeback reports success while moving nothing.
# Measured on vermeer / android13-5.15 5.15.216 with SELinux Enforcing: the
# ROM's own loop49 backing store had written 0 pages since boot (bd_stat
# 0 0 0), a manual 16 MiB budget moved exactly 0 pages, and the same writeback
# with the rule below in place moved exactly the 4096 pages of that budget.
#
# Least privilege on purpose: read and write are the only permissions the loop
# worker needs, because the kernel never resolves the path itself -- losetup
# opened the file in the root domain, and only I/O on the already-open file
# happens in the kernel domain.  A measured 32 MiB writeback-and-read-back
# cycle produces no surviving AVC with this rule and nothing else.
#
# The rule has to name the file TYPE of the backing store actually in use, and
# there is more than one in play on this device.  The module's own backing file
# lives under /data/per_boot/zram/ and carries zram_data_file; the ROM's memory
# extension (Xiaomi extm) backs its own loop device with
# /data/extm/extm_file, which carries extm_data_file instead.  A preserved ROM
# attachment is the case the module prefers (it never trades the ROM's device
# away), so that type has to be allowed too -- without it the rule above matches
# nothing on a device whose backing store is the ROM's, and every page still
# returns -EIO with bd_stat pinned at 0 0 0.  Measured on vermeer / 5.15.216
# 2026-09-15: /data/extm/extm_file is u:object_r:extm_data_file:s0 while
# /data/per_boot/zram is u:object_r:zram_data_file:s0.
#
# Adding a type that the ROM does not define is harmless: this ksud reports
# success for an unresolvable symbol anyway, so a stray allow() statement costs
# nothing on a ROM that has no extm.
allow kernel zram_data_file file { read write }
allow kernel extm_data_file file { read write }
